   "html_text": "A question Godfrey was asked in Quodlibet XI gave him the opportunity to profess his mature thought about political authority. He was asked whether the political leader can oblige his subjects to pay a tax for the utility of the state even if this utility is not manifest (q. 17). The historical context is a tax Philip the Fair raised to wage war. Godfrey premises his answer with reflections about political authority and good government. Since rulers should be the most virtuous and prudent, they ",
